<section class="sp-hero"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1774870719899-8c971956d685?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1600&h=760&q=75" alt="various spices and fruits arranged on a mat" width="1600" height="760" fetchpriority="high"><div class="sp-b"><div class="sp-hero-t"><p class="sp-kick">An independent spice companion</p><h1>A good spice rack is a small one, used often.</h1><p>Spicely helps you keep spices fresh, mix your own blends and rescue a recipe when a jar runs out. Three tools, two guides and no shopping basket.</p><p class="sp2-acts"><a class="btn" href="#tools">Open the spice tools</a><a class="sp2-ghost" href="spice-storage-guide.html">Storage guide</a></p></div></div></section>

<section class="sp-s"><div class="sp-b sp2-famw"><div><p class="sp-kick">Six families</p><h2>Every spice belongs to a flavour family</h2><p class="sp-sub">Learn the families and you can season by instinct: pick one or two from different groups and they will nearly always get along.</p><div class="sp-fam"><div class="f1"><h3>Warm</h3><p>Cinnamon, nutmeg, allspice, cloves</p></div><div class="f2"><h3>Earthy</h3><p>Cumin, turmeric, coriander seed</p></div><div class="f3"><h3>Hot</h3><p>Chilli, cayenne, black pepper, mustard</p></div><div class="f4"><h3>Sweet &amp; floral</h3><p>Cardamom, fennel, star anise, vanilla</p></div><div class="f5"><h3>Bright</h3><p>Sumac, dried lime, lemongrass, ginger</p></div><div class="f6"><h3>Herbal</h3><p>Oregano, thyme, bay, dried mint</p></div></div></div><figure><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1741521311950-fe32245b1e5d?auto=format&fit=crop&w=560&h=640&q=75" alt="shelves of spice jars on display" width="560" height="640" loading="lazy"></figure></div></section>

<section class="sp-s sp2-tools" id="tools"><div class="sp-b"><p class="sp-kick">Spice tools</p><h2>Three tools for your spice rack</h2><div class="sp2-cards">
<div class="sp2-card"><div><p class="sp-kick">Tool one</p><h3>Spice Blend Mixer</h3><p>Pick a blend and a batch size. You get the measures for each spice.</p><div class="sp-fl"><label for="bm-b">Blend<select id="bm-b"><option value="garam">Garam masala</option><option value="taco">Taco seasoning</option><option value="ras">Ras el hanout</option><option value="curry">Mild curry powder</option><option value="ital">Italian herb mix</option><option value="bake">Baking spice</option></select></label><label for="bm-n">Batch size (tablespoons)<input id="bm-n" type="number" min="1" max="24" value="4" inputmode="numeric"></label></div></div><div class="sp-res" id="bm-r" aria-live="polite"></div></div>
<div class="sp2-card"><div class="sp-res" id="sb-r" aria-live="polite"></div><div><p class="sp-kick">Tool two</p><h3>Substitute Finder</h3><p>Run out halfway through a recipe? Choose the missing spice.</p><div class="sp-fl"><label for="sb-s">I have run out of<select id="sb-s"><option value="cumin">Cumin</option><option value="coriander">Ground coriander</option><option value="paprika">Paprika</option><option value="smoked">Smoked paprika</option><option value="turmeric">Turmeric</option><option value="cinnamon">Cinnamon</option><option value="nutmeg">Nutmeg</option><option value="allspice">Allspice</option><option value="cardamom">Cardamom</option><option value="cayenne">Cayenne</option><option value="oregano">Oregano</option><option value="cloves">Cloves</option></select></label><label for="sb-q">The recipe asks for (teaspoons)<input id="sb-q" type="number" min="0.25" max="12" step="0.25" value="1" inputmode="decimal"></label></div></div></div>
<div class="sp2-card"><div><p class="sp-kick">Tool three</p><h3>Freshness Checker</h3><p>Decide whether a jar is worth keeping.</p><div class="sp-fl"><label for="fc-f">It is a<select id="fc-f"><option value="ground">Ground spice</option><option value="whole">Whole spice</option><option value="herb">Dried herb</option></select></label><label for="fc-m">Months since I opened it<input id="fc-m" type="number" min="0" max="120" value="14" inputmode="numeric"></label><label for="fc-s">It lives<select id="fc-s"><option value="1">In a dark cupboard</option><option value="1.3">On an open shelf</option><option value="1.6">Beside the cooker</option></select></label></div></div><div class="sp-res" id="fc-r" aria-live="polite"></div></div>
</div></div></section>

<section class="sp-s"><div class="sp-b"><p class="sp-kick">The first decision</p><h2>Whole or ground?</h2><div class="sp-vs"><div><h3>Whole</h3><ul><li>Keeps its flavour for three to four years</li><li>Can be toasted before grinding</li><li>Releases flavour slowly in stews and rice</li><li>Needs a grinder or a mortar</li></ul><p><b>Buy whole:</b> cumin, coriander, pepper, cardamom, nutmeg, cloves, fennel</p></div><span class="sp-vs-or">or</span><div><h3>Ground</h3><ul><li>Ready to use straight from the jar</li><li>Blends evenly into sauces and rubs</li><li>Fades within a year or two</li><li>Best bought in small jars</li></ul><p><b>Buy ground:</b> turmeric, paprika, ginger, cinnamon, cayenne, chilli powder</p></div></div></div></section>

<section class="sp-s sp-cream"><div class="sp-b sp-toast"><figure><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1768729340804-dd94f6dd612e?auto=format&fit=crop&w=640&h=720&q=75" alt="hands grinding spices in a bowl" width="640" height="720" loading="lazy"></figure><div><p class="sp-kick">Two minutes that change a dish</p><h2>How to toast and grind</h2><ol class="sp-steps"><li><span>Heat</span>Warm a dry frying pan over a medium flame. No oil.</li><li><span>Toast</span>Add whole spices in a single layer. Shake the pan for one to two minutes, until they smell nutty and a shade darker.</li><li><span>Cool</span>Tip onto a plate at once. They burn if left in the hot pan.</li><li><span>Grind</span>Crush in a mortar or a spice grinder, as fine as the dish needs.</li><li><span>Use</span>Add to the dish straight away, or store in a small jar for up to a month.</li></ol></div></div></section>

<section class="sp-s"><div class="sp-b"><p class="sp-kick">Read on</p><h2>The two guides</h2><div class="sp-gd"><a href="spice-storage-guide.html"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1781018727258-4a70d500e4ee?auto=format&fit=crop&w=640&h=400&q=75" alt="glass jars of garlic and onion granules" width="640" height="400" loading="lazy"><div><span>Guide I</span><h3>The Spice Storage Guide</h3><p>Where to keep spices, which jars to use, how long each kind lasts and how to clear out a neglected rack.</p></div></a><a href="spice-blends-guide.html"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1765117849007-10692cb39068?auto=format&fit=crop&w=640&h=400&q=75" alt="cinnamon sticks and star anise on a dark background" width="640" height="400" loading="lazy"><div><span>Guide II</span><h3>The Spice Blends Guide</h3><p>How a blend is built, six classic mixes with their ratios and how to adjust one to your own taste.</p></div></a></div></div></section>

<section class="sp-s sp-brown"><div class="sp-b sp-life"><div><p class="sp-kick">On the shelf</p><h2>How long spices keep</h2><p>These are flavour dates, not safety dates. An old spice will not harm you. It will only disappoint you.</p></div><div class="tblw"><table class="sp-tbl"><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>At its best</th><th>Still usable</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Whole seeds and bark</td><td>3 years</td><td>4 years</td></tr><tr><td>Whole dried chillies</td><td>1 year</td><td>2 years</td></tr><tr><td>Ground spices</td><td>1 year</td><td>2 years</td></tr><tr><td>Dried leafy herbs</td><td>1 year</td><td>2 years</td></tr><tr><td>Home-made blends</td><td>3 months</td><td>6 months</td></tr><tr><td>Toasted, ground at home</td><td>1 month</td><td>3 months</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></section>

<section class="sp-s"><div class="sp-b sp-pairs"><figure><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1727911583982-f3a5b2e2912a?auto=format&fit=crop&w=600&h=720&q=75" alt="cinnamon and star anise on a marble table" width="600" height="720" loading="lazy"></figure><div><p class="sp-kick">Reliable matches</p><h2>What goes with what</h2><dl class="sp-dl"><div><dt>Cumin</dt><dd>beans, lamb, carrots, tomatoes, yogurt</dd></div><div><dt>Coriander seed</dt><dd>chicken, fish, squash, citrus, lentils</dd></div><div><dt>Smoked paprika</dt><dd>potatoes, eggs, chickpeas, peppers</dd></div><div><dt>Turmeric</dt><dd>rice, cauliflower, lentils, fish</dd></div><div><dt>Cinnamon</dt><dd>apples, oats, lamb, aubergine, tomato sauces</dd></div><div><dt>Cardamom</dt><dd>rice, cream, coffee, pears, chicken</dd></div><div><dt>Fennel seed</dt><dd>pork, sausages, tomatoes, bread, fish</dd></div></dl></div></div></section>

<section class="sp-s sp-cream"><div class="sp-b sp-fqw"><p class="sp-kick">Asked in the kitchen</p><h2>Spice questions</h2><div class="sp-faq"><details open><summary>How long do ground spices last?</summary><p>Ground spices are at their best for about a year and fade noticeably after two. They do not become unsafe, only dull. Whole spices keep their flavour for three to four years because the oils stay locked inside.</p></details><details><summary>How can I tell if a spice is still good?</summary><p>Rub a pinch between your fingers and smell it. A fresh spice is strongly aromatic at once. If you have to search for the scent, or it smells dusty, it will add colour but very little flavour.</p></details><details><summary>Where is the best place to store spices?</summary><p>In airtight jars, in a cupboard or drawer away from the cooker, the kettle and direct sunlight. Heat, light, air and steam are what make spices fade.</p></details><details><summary>Should I buy whole or ground spices?</summary><p>Buy whole for the spices you use most and care most about, such as cumin, coriander, pepper and nutmeg, and grind small amounts as needed. Buy ground for spices that are hard to grind at home, such as turmeric, paprika and ginger.</p></details><details><summary>Why toast spices before using them?</summary><p>Gentle dry heat wakes up the aromatic oils and adds a nutty, rounded flavour. Toast whole spices in a dry pan for one to two minutes, until fragrant, then grind or add to the dish.</p></details><details><summary>When should spices go into the pan?</summary><p>Whole spices go in early, into hot oil, so their flavour carries through the dish. Ground spices follow the onions and cook for under a minute before liquid is added. Delicate dried herbs go in near the end.</p></details></div></div></section>

